From 739a5160a4ab56e146704e05d2d41fa0e6216a9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mokuroh54 <mokuroh54@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 16:26:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(training): validate --policy.type against the
 checkpoint's own config
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

788565c's guard compares the requested policy type against the source
JobRecord's recorded policy_type — MakerLab's bookkeeping about a checkpoint,
not the checkpoint itself. It is blind exactly where the defect bites: it only
runs under `if config.finetune_from_job_id:`, and it deliberately skips a
record whose type is blank or the "model" placeholder an import falls back to
when it could not read the checkpoint's config. That placeholder appears in
the same situation that leaves the UI's policy picker on its "act" default, so
the guard gives up precisely when the mismatch is most likely.

policy_pretrained_path is also a plain field of the public TrainingRequest and
server.py accepts a flat legacy body, so a caller can POST a pretrained path
with a contradicting policy_type and no finetune_from_job_id at all, bypassing
that guard entirely.

Add read_pretrained_policy_type(), which reads the `type` from the CHECKPOINT'S
OWN config.json (local dir, or Hub repo root via hf_hub_download), and
_check_pretrained_policy_type(), which raises ValueError (-> HTTP 400) only on
a provable contradiction. Wired into JobRegistry.start at a single choke point
covering every origin of a pretrained path, skipped on resume (that path passes
--config_path and never emits pretrained_path).

The two guards fail in opposite directions, so both are kept: guard 1 needs no
network and runs before the pretrained path resolves, and it still covers an
unreadable/private/offline checkpoint that the new check cannot inspect; the
new check covers a missing job id and a placeholder record. Only "no record AND
unreadable checkpoint" is left uncovered.

Also adds makerlab/finetune_audit.py: the detection logic as pure functions,
reporting a verdict AND a confidence, with is_clear() false for INDETERMINATE
so "cannot determine" can never be read as "fine". Deliberately not wired to
any endpoint or UI — an audit of this machine found zero damaged checkpoints
(no fine-tune was ever launched), so it exists tested and ready rather than
rendering on nothing.

Root cause remains upstream and is NOT fixed by this: policies/factory.py
make_policy sets kwargs["config"] = cfg explicitly, so from_pretrained skips
its `if config is None` branch and never reads the checkpoint's own config,
and it never passes strict — which defaults to False at
policies/pretrained.py:174. MakerLab shells out to lerobot_train and cannot
override it, so validating the pair before the spawn is the only local
equivalent.

KNOWN ISSUE, not addressed here: _check_pretrained_policy_type runs inside
JobRegistry's self._lock, so its hf_hub_download can hold the registry lock
across a network call with no explicit timeout. Harmless when cached or
offline-fast, but a slow Hub response stalls job listing, status polls and the
watchdog. The lookup needs nothing from the lock and should be hoisted out of
it.

Verified: 129 passed across test_finetune_audit/test_jobs/test_train/
test_training_preflight (+19 new); test_models.py unchanged at its 8-failure
environment baseline; ruff check clean; ruff format --check fails only on the
pre-existing jobs.py/test_jobs.py pair. No frontend changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
